  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate change is accelerating the frequency and intensity of extreme weather events, including droughts, floods, and cyclones. The compounding and cascading impacts from this have meant ever shorter cycles of disaster response and recovery, leading to narrower options for disaster preparedness.  The Australian Water Partnership has therefore partnered with The Pacific Community (SPC) to improve flood early warning systems supporting the increasingly important hydrological services across SPC member countries. 
&#x200d;
The aim is to embed key tools and practices, including the flood early warning tool that uses the UN Multi-Hazard Early Warning Framework. This tool helps to increase local hydrological monitoring capabilities through the installation of specialised and locally adapted equipment. It also supports accurate forecasting of flood impacts.  This work is likely to be adopted by the World Metrological Organization under its EW4All (Early Warnings for All) project.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian Water Partnership (AWP) works with governments across the Indo-Pacific to co-create and implement climate-resilient, sustainable, and inclusive water resource management solutions. Now in Phase 3, the partnership builds on its past success with a broad portfolio of activities to respond to water related crises made worse by climate change.  
&#x200d;
Responding to requests from partners, AWP supported climate-resilient water management policies, practices and tools through regional initiatives and in 12 countries in the Indo-Pacific. The partnership delivers over 60 diverse activities to strengthen water management capacity in the Pacific; support sustainable hydropower in Nepal and water infrastructure planning across the Mekong River Basin; improve industrial wastewater and water quality management in Indonesia, Lao PDR, Vietnam and Thailand; and help build inclusive water governance, policy and regulatory reform and community-based water resource management in PNG.  
&#x200d;
These activities, being demand-driven and highly context-specific, help catalyse sustained actions for climate resilient water.  They also present platforms to mobilise Australia&#x2019;s rich expertise in water management.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian government, through the Australian Water Partnership (AWP), has partnered with the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) to support Indonesia&#x2019;s food security and climate resilience by tackling worsening water scarcity. 
&#x200d;
Less predictable weather patterns due to climate change and greater demands from industry are threatening availability of water for agriculture. Working with government, farmers and water managers, the Water Scarcity Program first identified key policy, data and capacity gaps that hampered water management. To address these gaps, the partnership is strengthening local capacity in water accounting.  
&#x200d;
Through targeted engagement to build local ownership, the partnership has achieved a consensus for three new national evidence-based frameworks: the Water Scarcity Action Plan, Water Tenure Assessment, and Water Accounting Roadmap.  These sit alongside community level guidelines to implement water management improvements that support farmers, improve data feedback loops, and empower rural communities.  Water functions as a significant multiplier for local economies and AWP&#x2019;s support aligns well with the Indonesian government&#x2019;s aspiration to make Indonesia food secure.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
